response:
 The following CSS code is designed to give the image a more 3D appearance by adjusting the width and height of the element.  This code should also be placed within the body tag in order to apply it to all images on the page.  In this case, the "circle" class applies the changes to the entire image, while the "img" class applies them only to the central image area.  In addition, the CSS "transform" property is used to rotate the image around its center point.  

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
    <head>
        <title>Rotating Image</title>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <style type="text/css">
            .circle {
                width: 50px;
                height: 50px;
                transform: rotateZ(-90deg) translateY(0px);
                }

            .img {
                width: 50px;
                height: 50px;
                transform: rotateX(90deg) translateY(0px);
                }

        </style>
    </head>
    <body>
        <div id="chart-area"></div>

        <img src="images/logo.png" class="img" alt="Logo" />

        <img src="images/logo.png" class="circle" alt="Logo" />
    </body>
</html>

